---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Drip vs Postscript for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Drip vs Postscript for Shopify stores — visual email workflow control vs SMS-native specialization, stack architecture, compliance, and team fit.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Drip or Postscript better for a Shopify store doing $80k/mo?',
    answer: 'Revenue alone does not determine the stack. Evaluate email lifecycle ownership and the SMS use cases separately: welcome, cart, post-purchase and winback email, then permissioned text recovery and replies. Verify the current contracted channels and integrations in each account. Choose one or two tools only after checking workflow coverage, staffing and dispatch-time suppression; a revenue milestone is not evidence that both are needed.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Postscript replace Drip entirely?',
    answer: 'Compare the current contracted product scopes before deciding. Map every email journey, segment and integration you would lose or rebuild, and test it in the destination account. An SMS workflow is not an email replacement, and do not assume Drip includes a native SMS sender. Establish the actual sender and queue owner for each channel before switching traffic.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Drip vs Postscript pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Drip bills on people in account — typically $120–200/mo at 12k contacts. Postscript uses plan plus usage-based messaging — transparent for SMS-heavy stores but spikes during flash sales. Model promotional SMS volume explicitly for BFCM. Combined stack cost often exceeds either platform alone — justify with attributable SMS revenue.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use Drip and Postscript together?',
    answer: 'Use both only when each has a defined job and your team can coordinate them. Document which tool owns each email or SMS step, the permitted purpose and channel, and the current purchase, subscription and suppression state. Cancel queued recovery after purchase or opt-out, and deduplicate event replay using stable event identifiers. Tags alone do not establish SMS permission.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Drip or Postscript?',
    answer: 'Sequenzy handles email lifecycle with pay-per-email pricing — pair with Postscript for SMS depth. Drip still wins for visual workflow purists who want canvas control. Sequenzy plus Postscript is a lean stack for guided email strategy and specialized SMS without Drip maintenance overhead.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better TCPA compliance tooling?',
    answer: 'Evaluate the SMS controls actually available in the contracted account and destination regions: opt-in evidence, opt-out processing, quiet hours, reply handling and audit exports. Do not infer legal compliance from a vendor label or email permission. Current requirements and configuration need review for the proposed message purpose and recipients; product guardrails do not certify a campaign.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles cart abandonment better?',
    answer: 'Depends on channel. Drip email cart workflows excel at branching by discount sensitivity, purchase history, and tag logic — strong for education-first recovery on higher AOV. Postscript SMS cart recovery wins on immediacy for mobile-first flash drops — streetwear, limited releases, drop-day urgency. Best results combine Drip email with Postscript SMS, not either alone.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Build proposed tests rather than preselecting a winner: welcome branched by signup source, a three-step cart journey, and post-purchase cross-sell for email; permissioned recovery, quiet-hour scheduling, keyword opt-in and replies for SMS. Verify current plan and integration support first. Score event accuracy, suppression propagation, queue cancellation, replay deduplication and operator effort with synthetic profiles before any approved live experiment.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Drip vs Postscript</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Postscript for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
          Treat this as a proposed architecture evaluation, not a record of merchant tests. Compare Drip email workflow design with the current Postscript channel offering and the integrations available in your account. A hypothetical streetwear label could test drop alerts alongside email nurture; a supplements shop could map education and recovery; a skincare team could test category-based branching. These scenarios have no verified revenue or observed results. Confirm current product, plan and regional scope before deciding which system owns each journey.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">This comparison weights SMS-native compliance, quiet hours, cost-per-message economics, and whether each platform treats texts as scarce interrupts — not email with fewer characters. Sequenzy is evaluated as the lifecycle email layer beside dedicated SMS senders.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Email workflows', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Visual canvas for branching, tags, and behavior triggers.' },
            { label: 'SMS specialization', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Evaluate SMS controls, reply handling, and Shopify fit.' },
            { label: 'Operator control', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Hands-on logic without enterprise overhead.' },
            { label: 'SMS revenue focus', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'When text is a primary channel, not an add-on.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Email playbooks paired with Postscript for SMS depth.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Drip</th><th>Postscript</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Visual email workflow operators</td><td>SMS-native Shopify marketing</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>From ~$39/mo</td><td>Usage-based plans</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email</td><td>Core product</td><td>Not primary</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS depth</td><td>Verify current sender and integration scope</td><td>Evaluate current Shopify SMS scope</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Workflow UI</td><td>Visual canvas</td><td>SMS automations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Compliance</td><td>Solid for email</td><td>SMS-first guardrails</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify depth</td><td>Native — orders, tags, products</td><td>Native — SMS events</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical role</td><td>Primary email ESP</td><td>SMS layer in combined stack</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why this comparison matters for Shopify merchants</h2>
        <p>
          Drip and Postscript appear in retention-stack comparisons, but tool labels do not define the current contracted scope. Start with a journey map: email education, browse nurture, permissioned text alerts and reply handling. In a hypothetical streetwear shop, test whether separate tools give the operator useful control over post-purchase education and drop announcements. Do not presume that speed or a channel label predicts conversion.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a proposed supplements-shop evaluation, inventory welcome, post-purchase cross-sell and winback alongside cart recovery and keyword acquisition. Check that the intended destination supports each required channel, trigger and exclusion. Rehearse exports, field mappings and suppression propagation before switching; there is no verified eight-week migration or measured outcome behind this example.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: welcome and onboarding</h2>
        <p>
          For the welcome test, propose branches for popup versus footer signup and first product viewed. Verify that the required events, identifiers and integration actions exist in the current plan. Test SMS handoff only for profiles with permission for the intended text purpose; a tag is not consent. Record operator effort and event latency instead of promising an afternoon setup or declaring a winner from an assumed revenue mix.
        </p>
        <p>
          A hypothetical skincare team could make welcome email three reflect the first category viewed and invite a separate text opt-in for restock alerts. Verify category-event coverage and field mapping, then test whether permission and suppression propagate correctly. Assign the sending tool, queue owner and cancellation responsibility before seasonal changes. This is an evaluation scenario, not an observed successful handoff.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: abandoned cart</h2>
        <p>
          Drip's cart workflow showed exactly which branch fired for discount-sensitive tags — valuable when the streetwear label A/B tested education vs 10% off in step two. Cross-journey suppression — exclude shoppers in active winback — was tighter in Drip's visual rules after setup. Postscript cart texts fired faster on mobile — incremental recovery on drop-day urgency when email sat unopened for four hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed streetwear test, assign email steps one and three and an optional text step two only to shoppers permitted to receive that purpose and channel. Recheck purchase, subscription and suppression state immediately before dispatch. Document which sender cancels queued messages and how retries or replayed events are deduplicated; a shared list alone does not prove that cancellation has happened.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing reality for combined stacks</h2>
        <p>
          At 15k contacts, Drip typically lands $150–240/mo. Postscript adds plan plus per-message costs — moderate promotional volume can run $200–400/mo depending on broadcast frequency. Combined TCO exceeds either alone. Justify with attributable SMS revenue: if Postscript drives 20% of recovered carts at $95 AOV, the stack math works. If SMS is occasional, Drip-only or Drip plus lighter SMS may suffice.
        </p>
        <p>
          Flash sale weeks spike Postscript bills unpredictably. Budget BFCM SMS volume explicitly — the streetwear label modeled three broadcast tiers and set Postscript spend caps. Drip billing stays contact-based; list hygiene on popup imports matters before January renewal.
        </p>

        <h2>Shopify data depth in production</h2>
        <p>
          Evaluate Shopify event and field coverage with synthetic orders, customer tags and product changes. Check identifiers, delay semantics and regional or plan restrictions in each current integration. Then verify that each event reaches the intended workflow once, that opt-out propagates, and that a purchase cancels recovery still in the queue. Treat complementary channel roles as a hypothesis to validate, not evidence that every store needs two systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a hypothetical skincare catalog with replenishment intervals from 28 to 60 days, test product-specific delays and purchase exclusions using synthetic profiles. Restock subscription state and marketing permission need separate checks for the intended text. Compare education and urgency scenarios without asserting that a particular split performed better; the examples describe proposed tests rather than measured results.
        </p>

        <h2>Team fit: who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Drip if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your retention owner thinks in workflows and wants canvas visibility for email</li>
          <li>Custom branching by tags, custom fields, and purchase behavior is central</li>
          <li>You will A/B test email journey paths frequently</li>
          <li>SMS is secondary — or you will pair a dedicated SMS tool like Postscript</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Postscript if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your planned SMS use cases justify dedicated ownership after a matched evaluation</li>
          <li>Two-way conversations, keyword campaigns, and TCPA guardrails matter</li>
          <li>Drop-day urgency and mobile-first flash catalogs define your channel mix</li>
          <li>Email already lives in Drip, Klaviyo, or another ESP — Postscript is the SMS layer</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Also consider Sequenzy</h2>
        <p>
          If Drip's canvas feels like maintenance debt and you still need email depth alongside Postscript SMS, Sequenzy offers guided lifecycle playbooks with pay-per-email pricing and unlimited contacts. It will not replace Postscript's SMS specialization or Drip's visual workflow purism. It can replace the strategic blank page — what flows to run, in what order, with what suppression — while Postscript handles text urgency.
        </p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Drip wins email workflow control; Postscript wins SMS specialization. Most Shopify stores at scale need both in a coordinated stack — not a winner-take-all choice. Run the four-flow email test on Drip and the SMS compliance test on Postscript. If you are choosing only one platform, pick Drip for email-primary retention and add Postscript when SMS revenue justifies dedicated infrastructure. The supplements shop learned that lesson at migration cost.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-12">
      <h2>TCPA, consent, and cost-per-message discipline</h2>
      <p>SMS on Shopify is not a faster email channel. Every send needs express written consent, documented opt-in source, quiet-hour configuration, and a margin model that includes platform fees, carrier surcharges, and discount depth. Postscript, Attentive, and Omnisend SMS expose compliance tooling — but merchants still own consent copy at checkout, popups, and keyword campaigns.</p>
      <p>Model cost per delivered message before scaling broadcasts. A recovered cart that required 25% off plus three texts is often negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair urgent SMS with Sequenzy email when the message needs education, social proof, or multi-product context — never duplicate the same offer on both channels within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <p>Weekly ops: audit STOP/HELP handling, reply inbox backlog, opt-out spikes by flow, and suppression overlap with email cart recovery. Quarterly: re-read popup and checkout consent language against live automations. TCPA litigation and carrier filtering both punish stores that treat SMS like promotional email with shorter copy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>SMS operations depth 1: keyword opt-in campaigns audited…</h2>
      <p>For <strong>compare drip vs postscript</strong>, keyword opt-in campaigns audited for disclosure drift is not optional infrastructure. Shopify SMS apps that recover urgent revenue without wrecking consent treat every text as a scarce interrupt — cart recovery after meaningful checkout intent, launch access for opted-in VIPs, replenishment when SKU usage data supports timing.</p>
      <p>Postscript remains the SMS-native benchmark on this site: TCPA tooling, two-way replies, Shopify event triggers, and broadcast controls. Sequenzy is deliberately ranked as lifecycle email layer beside SMS — welcome depth, post-purchase education, winback arcs that should not be crammed into 160 characters. Klaviyo and Omnisend fit when one team wants bundled SMS with segmentation; Attentive when enterprise acquisition economics justify custom contracts.</p>
      <p>Run the math before scaling: platform fee plus carrier fee plus average discount per recovered order. A text that needs 25% off to convert may be negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair channels deliberately — email for proof and education, SMS for timing — and never duplicate the same offer within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Document opt-in source, message type promised, and maximum weekly frequency per segment.</li>
        <li>Configure quiet hours before enabling automations; audit checkout and popup copy quarterly.</li>
        <li>Assign one owner for reply inbox, suppression maps, and pre-launch TCPA review.</li>
        <li>Measure opt-out rate by flow, cost per incremental order, and full-price repeat purchase quality.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>SMS operations depth 2: TCPA express written consent…</h2>
      <p>For <strong>compare drip vs postscript</strong>, TCPA express written consent and opt-in source documentation is not optional infrastructure. Shopify SMS apps that recover urgent revenue without wrecking consent treat every text as a scarce interrupt — cart recovery after meaningful checkout intent, launch access for opted-in VIPs, replenishment when SKU usage data supports timing.</p>
      <p>Postscript remains the SMS-native benchmark on this site: TCPA tooling, two-way replies, Shopify event triggers, and broadcast controls. Sequenzy is deliberately ranked as lifecycle email layer beside SMS — welcome depth, post-purchase education, winback arcs that should not be crammed into 160 characters. Klaviyo and Omnisend fit when one team wants bundled SMS with segmentation; Attentive when enterprise acquisition economics justify custom contracts.</p>
      <p>Run the math before scaling: platform fee plus carrier fee plus average discount per recovered order. A text that needs 25% off to convert may be negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair channels deliberately — email for proof and education, SMS for timing — and never duplicate the same offer within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Document opt-in source, message type promised, and maximum weekly frequency per segment.</li>
        <li>Configure quiet hours before enabling automations; audit checkout and popup copy quarterly.</li>
        <li>Assign one owner for reply inbox, suppression maps, and pre-launch TCPA review.</li>
        <li>Measure opt-out rate by flow, cost per incremental order, and full-price repeat purchase quality.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>




  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
